Quoting  Byron A Jeff <byron@cc.gatech.edu>:
>David Damerell  <damerell@chiark.greenend.org.uk> wrote:
>>The question is - since you are obviously very good at the early game,
>>what would your percentage be if you didn't do the racket and spent equal
>>time developing skill at that approach?
>The real questions are does doing the run mean that I'm never going to buy
>protection? Or never go to Minetown? Minetown is such a rich target in the
>upper dungeon, virtually every nethack character is going to get there.

Indeed. But would your percentage be higher if you went there without
explicitly considering the racket - but still sucking money out of shops,
etc? We don't know that, and that's the question.

For example, in the 2004 Tournament (when I think I was playing at the
highest standard I have yet managed), I had 3 ascensions, one
killed-on-the-Quest, one killed mounting the original pony, one killed
post-Minetown by an ettin mummy, one killed by a watchman (oops).

The pony stupidity was independent of the racket. The Quest death already
had maxed protection. Logically I could have managed the watchman-angering
while racketing. That leaves the ettin mummy. That character bought quite
a lot of protection anyway, being an archaeologist. A few points of extra
AC might have saved him, so there's certainly a benefit to the MPR there.
On the other hand, that pony aside, I was reaching Minetown 100% without
the racket. If the percentage chance of the unavoidable deaths sjdevnull
mentions is high enough, the MPR might well be a net penalty to a player
of the skill I had then.

>The MPR is just a systematic way of exploiting the resources that the game
>offers in the upper dungeon. Characters come out the back end with a ton
>of stuff, and added protection.

Again you're attributing things which are nothing to do with the MPR to
it. That ton of stuff is equally available either way.
